<article class="env-lemma" id="0BY5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BY5">Lemma <span data-tag="0BY5">53.6.1</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a proper scheme over $k$ having dimension $1$ and $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X) = k$. Then $X$ is connected, Cohen-Macaulay, and equidimensional of dimension $1$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $\Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X) = k$ has no nontrivial idempotents, we see that $X$ is connected. This already shows that $X$ is equidimensional of dimension $1$ (any irreducible component of dimension $0$ would be a connected component). Let $\mathcal{I} \subset \mathcal{O}_ X$ be the maximal coherent submodule supported in closed points. Then $\mathcal{I}$ exists (Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/02OL" data-tag="02OL">31.4.6</a>) and is globally generated (Varieties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AYT" data-tag="0AYT">33.33.3</a>). Since $1 \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ is not a section of $\mathcal{I}$ we conclude that $\mathcal{I} = 0$. Thus $X$ does not have embedded points (Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/02OL" data-tag="02OL">31.4.6</a>). Thus $X$ has $(S_1)$ by Divisors, Lemma <a href="/tag/0346" data-tag="0346">31.4.3</a>. Hence $X$ is Cohen-Macaulay. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>